From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 19:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] shared/netutils/dhcpserver: answer only on the interface that
 owns the server's address.

Each DHCP server bound its socket to every interface. With the Wi-Fi access
point and USB networking both up, each server answered on both, so a Wi-Fi
client could get a USB network address (169.254.x.y) instead of one from
the access point's 192.168.4.0/24. The second server could not even bind
port 67, so the first one answered everywhere. Both servers now share the
port, and each binds its socket to the netif that holds the address it
serves.
---
 shared/netutils/dhcpserver.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/shared/netutils/dhcpserver.c b/shared/netutils/dhcpserver.c
--- a/shared/netutils/dhcpserver.c
+++ b/shared/netutils/dhcpserver.c
@@ -300,7 +300,23 @@
     if (dhcp_socket_new_dgram(&d->udp, d, dhcp_server_process) != 0) {
         return;
     }
+    // The server on the other interface already holds port 67, and lwIP
+    // refuses a second bind unless both sockets allow sharing it. That
+    // refusal is why only one server ever answered.
+    ip_set_option(d->udp, SOF_REUSEADDR);
     dhcp_socket_bind(&d->udp, 0, PORT_DHCP_SERVER);
+
+    // Answer only on the interface that owns the server's address. Without
+    // this, a broadcast that arrives on any other interface reaches the server
+    // too, and a second server on that interface hands out leases from the
+    // wrong network.
+    struct netif *netif;
+    NETIF_FOREACH(netif) {
+        if (ip_addr_eq(&netif->ip_addr, &d->ip)) {
+            udp_bind_netif(d->udp, netif);
+            break;
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 void dhcp_server_deinit(dhcp_server_t *d) {
